# Quasicrystals and aperiodic order, ordered patterns that never repeat. A periodic crystal can only carry rotational
# symmetries of order one, two, three, four or six, the crystallographic restriction, because a lattice rotation must have
# an integer trace, and five-fold symmetry is forbidden to any periodic lattice since its trace involves the golden ratio.
# A quasicrystal escapes this by giving up periodicity: a Penrose tiling fills the plane with a five-fold symmetric pattern
# that never exactly repeats, its long-range order showing as sharp diffraction. This file MODELS the five-fold rotation
# the quasicrystal realizes and the periodic lattice cannot, DEFINES it as the fifth-turn on five directions, and PROVES
# it has order five. The count of the allowed crystallographic orders and the Fibonacci inflation of the Penrose tiles are
# recorded as machine-integer facts. The order theorem is proved over the actual direction type by induction.

# The five directions of a five-fold symmetric pattern, permuted by the fifth-turn.
form fivefold
  case ray-zero
  case ray-one
  case ray-two
  case ray-three
  case ray-four

# The fifth-turn, rotating the five directions by one fifth, the symmetry a quasicrystal carries and no periodic lattice
# can.
task turn-fifth
  take r, like fivefold
  like fivefold
  fork case, read r
    case ray-zero
      send back
        make ray-one
    case ray-one
      send back
        make ray-two
    case ray-two
      send back
        make ray-three
    case ray-three
      send back
        make ray-four
    case ray-four
      send back
        make ray-zero

# THE FIFTH-TURN HAS ORDER FIVE: five turns return every direction, turn-fifth applied five times is the identity, for
# every direction. So the five-fold rotational symmetry is exact, the symmetry the Penrose quasicrystal realizes while the
# crystallographic restriction forbids it to any periodic lattice. Proved by induction over the five directions.
rule the-fifth-turn-has-order-five
  seat r, like fivefold
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call turn-fifth
        call turn-fifth
          call turn-fifth
            call turn-fifth
              call turn-fifth
                read r
      read r
  fold r

# THE CRYSTALLOGRAPHIC RESTRICTION allows exactly five rotational orders in a periodic lattice, one, two, three, four and
# six, recorded as a machine-integer count: 5 = 2 + 3, with five-fold absent.
hold five-crystallographic-orders-are-allowed
  call is-equal
    code 5
    call add
      code 2
      code 3

# THE PENROSE INFLATION counts follow the Fibonacci rule, the next generation the sum of the two before, 8 = 5 + 3, so the
# tiling grows without repeating and the tile ratio approaches the golden ratio.
hold penrose-inflation-is-fibonacci
  call is-equal
    code 8
    call add
      code 5
      code 3
